nt  for decades we've trailed our  competitors  in france businesses invest around 30  more relative to gdp every year  if we could just close this gap we would  land a serious blow in our battle  against low growth  now we know it requires public  investment  that's why we have our climate  investment pledge  we know it requires fair taxation  which is why we will scrap business  rates and replace them with a system  that levels the playing field  but we also know  it requires stability  and that requires institutions  that take a serious  patient  long-term view about what needs to be  done  so today i can announce we will  establish an industrial strategy council  and we will go further by putting it on  a statutory footing  it will provide advice that shapes  policy  in the way that climate change committee  does or the office of budget  responsibility  a permanent part of the landscape  that sets out our strategic national  priorities  that go beyond the political cycle  brings in the expertise of business  science and unions  holds us to account for our decisions  and builds confidence for investors  that will boost long-term growth and  productivity  my fourth and fifth principles are best  taken together  because spreading power and raising the  productivity of the economy everywhere  are fundamental to growing our  contribution  on spreading power i've asked gordon  brown to look at new forms of economic  devolution  to make sure the decisions about things  that drive regional growth  like skills  infrastructure attracting investment are  all made by people with skin in the game  labor will not attempt to run our  leveling up strategy from the center  nor will we offer it alongside a  divisive argument about north versus  south  city versus town to me they've always  felt like false choices  a productive liverpool  is good for birkenau  a strong dudley  is good for birmingham  and each has a shared fate  in the wider battle against regional  inequality  but we must also recognize  that every place needs the power to grow  its own economy  so our reforms will allow devolved and  local government to make long-term  financial decisions  to reap the rewards of investment in  their economy  that way you make sure that every city  every town every place  takes ownership  of their contribution  that people  and businesses with a long-term  commitment to their community  work together  in partnership  it's what labour is delivering in power  and it's what labor will deliver in  national government  but as well spreading power  real leveling up also requires  investment  and this is where my labour party will  move on from the old ideas  because the old approach focused on  growing the pie in any way possible  then redistributing  but this is not strong secure  and fair growth  it leaves too many people in insecure  jobs  where their hard work is not fairly  rewarded  too many communities locked out from the  benefits of growth  and redistribution  can't repair the contract  now there's no point looking to the  right on this  the evidence of the past decade shows  they will only give us stagnation  but what we will do  what winning center-left parties around  the world have done  is to adopt a new approach to investment  and approach the us treasury secretary  janet yellen has called modern  supply-side economics  it rests  in part  on a universal truth about social  democracy  a strong economy needs strong public  services  and strong public services need reform  and investment  but it also rests on something more  subtle  because the investments we make now  should have a laser-like focus on  boosting long-term productivity across  the country  not just quick winds  in the southeast  which means we must learn to focus on  the supply side  on growing the collective contribution  everywhere  now you can see this in our approach to  fully funded plans for public service  reform  whether it's our 8500 new mental health  professionals  or our national excellence program for  schools that will tackle  the educational inequality at the heart  of low productivity  our policies are not just an investment  in the health and well-being of our  community  they are also an investment in the  long-term growth and productivity of the  country  there's no tension here  just look at how many people at the  moment can't work  because of their health  a highly skilled and healthy nation and  a fast-growing modern economy depend on  each other  i never think it's too hard to identify  what people want from politics  at the moment it's probably easier than  usual  people want a fresh start for britain  they want the opportunity to get on  and above all they want to be free from  the insecurity of the cost of living  crisis  the approach to growth i've set out  today will challenge my party's instinct  it pushes us to care as much about  growth and productivity as we have done  in the past about redistribution and  investment  not to hark back to our old ideas in the  face of new challenges  you will see a clear contrast between my  labor party and the thatcherite cosplay  on display tonight  the difference between a labour party  ready to take britain forward  and a tory party that wants to take us  back to the past  between labor growth  tory stagnation  that will be the choice at the next  election and we are ready  ready to renew the contract with working  people  ready to reboot our economy and end the  cost of living crisis  ready to unlock the contribution of  every business every person and every  community  and deliver the strong secure and fair  growth our country needs thank you very  much  thank you  i think we've got a number of questions  starting with erica  you mentioned liz trust and mushy soon a  few times then isn't the problem that  tory leadership contest is just drowning  out you and any policies you might want  to announce this summer  no i don't think that at all i think  what we're seeing from the leadership  contest is  um  two individuals who are part of the  problem we've had 12 years now of  stagnation they voted for every one of  those 15 tax rises and now they're  trying to distance themselves as quickly  as possible from it so  their answers if you like our  are too small for our country for the  challenge that we face and that's why  i've set out a strategic plan for the  future  and  you know i think the change we need now  is much more fundamental than a change  at the top of the tory party we need to  change a government and fresh start for  britain thank you erica elena  hi eleanor from the press association um  what exactly is your position on public  ownership of energy and water firms you  appear to have ruled out nationalization  yet you still have some interest in  common ownership can you explain what  that means well look i take a pragmatic  approach rather than an ideological one  agree with what rachel reeve said this  morning  having come through the pandemic  it's very important we have very very  clear priorities  and that's why we've set out fiscal  rules already as an opposition rachel  did that at uh conference last year  that's way ahead of the general election  setting out our priorities and my  priority as i hope is obvious from this  morning is growth  the mission of the next labor government  will be growth  and you know that  partnership with business is where i see  that growth coming from so my approach  here is is pragmatic not ideological my  mission is growth and underpinning that  mission is a partnership arrangement  with business where the mission is set  by an incoming labour government and we  empower business to work with us on  delivering on that mission  thank you eleanor i think we've got mark  from the guardian  hi um  hi um  you talked about uh uh sort of um  changing position uh not being trapped  in  uh parties history  uh  more focused on growth and productivity  but didn't the labour party didn't blair  and gordon brown's labour party  concentrate on growth and productivity  or did they  let it fall through the gaps oh they did  concentrate on growth and productivity  and  let's sum it up if the economy in the  last 12 years on the tourists had grown  at the same rate as it did under the  last labour government we would have  billions of pounds to spend on our  public services what i want to do is  build on that and take it forward  and to get away from the idea that  redistribution of the pie is all you  need to grow your economy all you need  for fairness what i want is growth so  that everyone everywhere can make a  contribution that is  for them their place their town their  village  their city and get a fair reward for  that contribution so it's to take that  forward but the answer to every question  about the economy has to be growth first  and you can't go to tax and spend until  you've answered the growth question we  put a serious answer to the growth  question on the table this morning  contrast that to the fantasy economics  going on in the tory leadership race  where they're making unfunded spending  commitments the magic monetary is  working overtime  with us you've got a serious plan for  the future and then i think i've got  liam  hi liam hi  yeah welcome to liverpool i don't know  if you've er ever got a train across the  north but i haven't been many times if  you have you may not have arrived at  your destination in some time um just to  push you on this on this public  ownership issue it seems like some  members of the shadow cabinet have  maintained that particularly when it  comes to rail the public ownership of  bringing the trains back into public  control remains a labor priority um can  i push you on that one please yeah  firstly i have taken that trade many  many times  because  before i was a politician i was director  of public prosecutions and i had staff  um in a hundred offices across the whole  of the united kingdom in including here  down the liverpool in there  and so i've done those journeys between  our cities and places  across the northeast the north and the  northwest many many times and absolutely  understand  why people feel so let down by a  government that promised to change that  um with the rail system that they  promised for the north and have  absolutely failed to deliver on it but  whether it comes to rail or anything  else liam  i want to be pragmatic about this rather  than ideological  you know i think  what some of our mayors and metro mayors  are doing with public transport is the  right way forward absolutely focus  on um keeping the price down and making  sure there's control over where uh  things go particularly buses recently  steve roberts here we had a very good  discussion recently about what we do  with our buses and i think that is the  difference that labour mayor's metro  mayors can make in power but liam as i  say pragmaticalness  rather than ideological  but i absolutely understand the  frustrations of everybody trying to use  the rail service particularly going  across the country rather than up and  down the country it's been  broken for a very long time the  government very very clear commitment to  it including a new line  and they've broken all of those  commitments and that is the  story of the last 12 years empty  promises  um never really meant  always broken and there are just so many  examples of it it's why the tory party  is in such a mess it's why the change we  need for our country is not just to  change the person at the top of the tory  party they're the problem they're not  the solution  we need a general election we need a  change of government thank you liam  thank you everybody for coming this  morning thank you  you
 

